  "letter": {
    "kind": "complaint",
    "to": "privacy@glencore.com",
    "subject": "Concern regarding cookie rejection and transparency on glencore.com",
    "body": "Dear Data Protection Officer,\n\nI am writing to raise concerns identified during an automated, passive privacy audit of https://glencore.com on 2026-11-08 04:07 UTC. The audit used a fresh browser, clicked the cookie banner\u2019s \u201cReject all cookies\u201d option, then recorded what was still sent while browsing the site.\n\nAfter rejecting cookies, the following pages were visited: https://www.glencore.com/en/careers, https://www.glencore.com/publications, and https://www.glencore.com/world-map. Your privacy notice at https://www.glencore.com/en/terms/privacy-notice was also reviewed.\n\nThe points I ask you to explain are:\n\n1. It was observed that Google still received data after cookies were rejected. Specifically, there were 7 requests to googletagmanager.com after \u201cReject all cookies\u201d was clicked. These were classified in the audit as advertising-related.\n\n2. Our review suggests that advertising tracking appears to operate despite cookie rejection. The audit successfully clicked \u201cReject all cookies\u201d, but Google advertising-related requests continued afterwards. This appears inconsistent with the statement in your policy that: \u201cWe take care to protect your information (even if it was collected by other organisations for example by having contracts that limit how your information can be used), and regularly check that they only use cookies that we have allowed.\u201d\n\n3. Our review also suggests that the privacy notice lacks substantive transparency information. Although it contains headings for legal basis, sharing, international transfers, retention and rights, it does not appear to provide substantive explanation under those headings, making it unclear how key aspects of personal data use are explained to individuals.\n\nPlease would you:\n\n1. Explain each of the points above.\n2. Confirm which cookies or trackers you consider strictly necessary or otherwise exempt, and why.\n3. Stop any non-essential tracking that operates without consent.\n4. Reply within one month.\n\nIf this matter is not resolved, it may be raised with the Information Commissioner\u2019s Office.\n\nYours faithfully,\nJoshua Baker",
